WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.970 --> 00:00:03.040
This is a shot from
Netflix's "Over the Moon"

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:04.750
while the film was still in production

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:07.623
as Fei Fei and her pet bunny,
Bungee, fly to the moon.

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:11.630
And here's the final
version you see in the film.

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:13.110
You'll notice that Fei Fei's skin

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:14.740
now looks more like human skin

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:16.160
and the lights from her control pad

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:17.710
properly reflect off her helmet,

00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:20.340
making the shield appear more like glass.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:22.940
The one ingredient responsible
for this transformation?

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:25.590
Lighting. But lighting 3D-animated movies

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:27.100
isn't only about the decisions

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:29.090
that make scenes look more realistic.

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:31.050
It's also about the emotional choices

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:33.500
that turn this image
into something magical.

00:00:36.795 --> 00:00:38.760
Fei Fei: I did it! I did it!

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:41.980
Narrator: So, how exactly does
lighting in animation work?

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:44.707
And, warning, spoilers
ahead for "Over the Moon."

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:50.360
Lighting is one of the final
elements added in 3D animation,

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:52.670
but animators have to
think about it early on.

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:54.350
A lighting artist's decisions are guided

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:56.310
by blueprints called color scripts,

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:58.370
which come before anything is animated.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:00.020
These are hand-painted pictures

00:01:00.020 --> 00:01:02.790
that break down an animated
film scene by scene.

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:04.470
By viewing them all next to each other,

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:06.240
the director and production designer

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.280
can break down the emotional story arc.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:09.730
And lighting here is key.

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:12.690
Celine Desrumaux: I'm trying
to see in the big story.

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:15.830
Which is the lowest point?
Like, the saddest moment?

00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:18.450
Which is a more action moment?

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:20.520
Which is a more funny moment

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:22.600
or the most extravagant moment?

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:23.820
Narrator: That's Celine Desrumaux,

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:25.900
the production designer
for "Over the Moon."

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:27.770
Celine used color scripts to decide

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:29.030
how they'd create a contrast

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:31.800
between Earth and the
moon kingdom of Lunaria.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.500
She found that by making the
colors on Earth less saturated,

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.267
the colors of Lunaria, with all of its

00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:38.170
light-emitting structures and characters,

00:01:38.170 --> 00:01:39.640
would look even bolder.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.600
Later on, the color scripts
get redefined into a color key,

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:43.750
which tells the lighting team

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:46.320
how light or dark a given
scene or shot should be

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.280
while also identifying the exact number

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:50.340
of lights, light sources, colors,

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:53.290
and even how much shadow
your subject is casting.

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:56.070
All of this information is
necessary for Clara Chan,

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:58.300
a CG supervisor who led the lighting team

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:00.540
at Sony Pictures Imageworks
for "Over the Moon."

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:03.760
Clara Chan: Pretty much
we control the intensity,

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:07.350
the placement of the lights, the colors.

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:08.940
Narrator: While lighting
in live-action movies

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:11.360
illuminates what's already
physically there on screen,

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:13.800
lighting animated movies
works a bit differently.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.300
Without it, the physical appearance

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:18.070
of what we see on screen
is completely altered

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:19.700
and won't look as realistic.

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:21.660
This happens in a few different ways.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:23.660
First is a process called surfacing,

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:25.640
which determines an object's texture

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:28.340
and how an object will
appear once light hits it.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:29.880
Things like roughness and shine

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:31.470
are decided through surfacing.

00:02:31.470 --> 00:02:33.770
To put it simply, surfacing
artists will assign

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:36.420
numerical values to something
like Fei Fei's jacket,

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:38.720
perhaps a high value for reflectiveness

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:40.960
and a low value for, say, roughness,

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:42.010
but we won't be able to see

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:43.760
what those values do to the surface

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:46.320
until lighting artists
actually add the light.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:49.350
To see just how surfacing works
to make a scene more real,

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:51.670
let's take a closer look
at the characters' skin.

00:02:51.670 --> 00:02:53.570
Before the lighting is added in this shot,

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:55.840
Fei Fei and Chin's skin looks opaque.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:57.590
Once bright yellow light is added,

00:02:57.590 --> 00:03:00.120
the shot not only becomes
much more colorful,

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:01.600
but their skin is also taking in

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:02.998
some of that color and light.

00:03:02.998 --> 00:03:06.200
Chan: Human skin is translucent,

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:10.660
and when light gets in,
it bounces around inside,

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:13.870
so you get this glowiness.

00:03:13.870 --> 00:03:15.560
Narrator: The subtle
glow throughout the skin

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:18.870
makes it look like real
skin instead of plastic.

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:21.100
But making an animated surface look good

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:24.580
also requires figuring out how
light behaves in real life.

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:26.470
Animators place many lights by hand,

00:03:26.470 --> 00:03:28.720
but there can be so much
light in a given shot

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:30.540
that computers need to
lend a helping hand.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:33.040
3D animators do this using ray tracing,

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:35.840
a rendering method that requires
a lot of computing power

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:38.590
in which the virtual camera
shoots out a light-sensing ray

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:40.430
until it hits an object in the scene.

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:41.910
If the object is transparent,

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:43.130
the light will travel through it.

00:03:43.130 --> 00:03:44.800
And if the object is reflective,

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.710
the light will bounce off at an angle.

00:03:46.710 --> 00:03:49.130
They can also use the more
advanced path tracing,

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:51.320
which sends out multiple rays at once.

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:54.040
As you can see, instead of
going right to the light source,

00:03:54.040 --> 00:03:55.490
the rays bounce around the room,

00:03:55.490 --> 00:03:57.180
hitting every possible angle

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:58.960
until they locate the light source.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:01.550
As the light bounces
around during path tracing,

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:04.160
subtle shadows and
reflections are produced.

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:06.070
Take a look at this shot
from "Incredibles 2,"

00:04:06.070 --> 00:04:07.820
which takes place in a school hallway,

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:09.200
so the primary light sources

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:10.970
are these overhead fluorescent lights.

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:12.450
The rays bouncing around the scene

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:15.160
determine the shadows cast by
these background characters,

00:04:15.160 --> 00:04:17.200
the shadow of Violet's
hair on her shoulders,

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:20.340
and even the reflection seen
on this clock behind Tony.

00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:23.230
Path tracing is why newer
animated films look so real

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:24.780
compared to older animations,

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:27.500
which didn't have the
processing power we do today.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:29.700
It gets more complicated
when characters and objects

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:31.400
are lit by multiple light sources,

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:33.740
like the scenes where Fei
Fei and her extended family

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:35.510
celebrate the Mid-Autumn Festival.

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:36.918
Here, there's some light coming from

00:04:36.918 --> 00:04:38.001
the kitchen behind them,

00:04:38.001 --> 00:04:40.030
but mainly from the
lanterns above their head.

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:41.780
That means the computer
has to chart the path

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:44.240
of each ray of light coming
from each light source

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:45.770
that makes it to the camera's lens.

00:04:45.770 --> 00:04:48.570
Chan: There is the
moonlight also in there,

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:51.750
but in a lesser intensity

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:56.490
so we could feel the actual
lanterns hanging above them.

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:57.580
Narrator: As Fei Fei goes inside,

00:04:57.580 --> 00:04:59.430
you can see her bangs cast shadows.

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:01.630
The computer calculated
that the shadow of her bangs

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:03.200
could cover a lot of her eyes,

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:04.670
so the lighting team made the decision

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:06.230
to break the rules of reality

00:05:06.230 --> 00:05:08.250
and eliminate or lighten
them when necessary

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:11.120
to help the audience better
see her facial expressions.

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:13.735
But it's not just up to computers
to make light look real.

00:05:13.735 --> 00:05:16.350
Lighting artists need to study
how light acts in reality

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:17.600
by observing the real thing,

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:19.670
like that in order to
see the surface of water,

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:21.200
you need a reflection.

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:22.640
This is exactly what Pixar did

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:25.070
when creating the underwater
world of "Finding Nemo."

00:05:25.070 --> 00:05:26.986
According to Pixar director of photography

00:05:26.986 --> 00:05:28.586
Danielle Feinberg, the team took

00:05:28.586 --> 00:05:30.300
a real clip of underwater footage,

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:32.300
recreated it in the
computer to break it down,

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:34.860
and found its most
prominent feature was light.

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:37.600
It impacts everything,
from visibility to color.

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:39.370
They found that objects
lose their full color

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:40.990
as they travel further into the water.

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:42.990
Meanwhile, these light
beams help guide the eye,

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:44.440
showing the viewer which way is up

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:46.420
in shots where the surface is invisible.

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:48.710
But through rendering,
these subtle light features,

00:05:48.710 --> 00:05:51.220
known as caustics, showed
up on the ocean floor.

00:05:51.220 --> 00:05:52.410
It's one of the many ways light

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:54.720
realistically reacts
to water in the movie.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:56.530
But lighting's purpose isn't just about

00:05:56.530 --> 00:05:59.150
physically improving the
look of an animated project.

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:01.530
It also helps set the
mood and emotional arc

00:06:01.530 --> 00:06:03.710
established by the color scripts.

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:06.550
While "Finding Nemo" created
nearly photorealistic water,

00:06:06.550 --> 00:06:08.650
it also defied those same rules

00:06:08.650 --> 00:06:10.480
to make a scene more emotional.

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:12.220
Danielle found that one
of the lighting elements

00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:14.450
they had the most leeway with was color.

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:17.560
So while the water in Sydney
Harbor is actually turquoise,

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:20.760
the lighting artists instead
made the water mucky and green

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:23.500
to represent one of the
film's emotional low points.

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:25.330
In that "Incredibles 2" hallway shot,

00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:26.163
they weren't just looking

00:06:26.163 --> 00:06:27.920
at how light realistically behaves.

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:30.230
According to Pixar lighting
artist Charu Clark,

00:06:30.230 --> 00:06:31.500
the fluorescent lights created

00:06:31.500 --> 00:06:33.430
a sterile and uncomfortable feeling,

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:35.360
which mirrored how
Violet felt at the moment

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:37.730
her crush couldn't remember who she was.

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:40.530
Later in the movie when Tony
finally remembers Violet,

00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:42.810
the same location is lit
up with bright sunlight

00:06:42.810 --> 00:06:45.750
to evoke a happy ending and the
two characters' fresh start.

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:47.800
Different lighting for a different mood.

00:06:47.800 --> 00:06:50.880
Meanwhile, when Fei Fei and
Chin first arrived on Lunaria,

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:51.950
it wasn't just about making

00:06:51.950 --> 00:06:53.695
their skin and outfits stand out more.

00:06:53.695 --> 00:06:55.770
The filmmakers wanted
to create a sense of awe

00:06:55.770 --> 00:06:58.498
and an overwhelming feeling
of wonder for the audience.

00:06:58.498 --> 00:07:01.060
So they filled this shot with
bright, glowing yellow light

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:02.833
emitted from the structure around them.

00:07:02.833 --> 00:07:05.070
Desrumaux: I wanted to, like, keep a space

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:06.700
for the audience to be amazed

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:08.400
for when they're going to the moon.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:09.870
Narrator: And during those family dinners,

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:12.600
especially the one after Fei
Fei returns from Lunaria,

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:15.090
they wanted to create a
warm and festive mood.

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:17.320
So even though there's light
from the moon and the kitchen,

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:18.330
the lighting artists chose

00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:20.180
to amplify the light from the lanterns

00:07:20.180 --> 00:07:22.380
to set the right emotion for the scene.

